---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { loadContent } from "../../lib/content";
import { calBookingUrl } from "../../lib/site";
import TutorTrialCallout from "../../components/TutorTrialCallout.astro";

const { provider } = loadContent();
---

<Layout
  title={`Electricity | CBSE Class 10 Science | ${provider.name}`}
  description="Complete CBSE Class 10 Science chapter on Electricity. Covers electric current, potential difference, Ohm's law, resistance, series and parallel circuits, Joule's heating effect, electric power, and interactive quiz."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="small" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/subjects/">Subjects</a> / <a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / Electricity
    </nav>

    <div class="resource-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">CBSE Class 10 · Science · Physics</span>
      <h1>Electricity</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Complete chapter notes, formulas, visual circuit diagrams, and an interactive quiz for CBSE Class 10 Physics: from electric current and Ohm's law to resistor combinations and electric power.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Key concepts -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Key Concepts at a Glance</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>⚡ Electric Current &amp; Charge</h3>
          <p class="formula">I = Q / t &nbsp;|&nbsp; Q = n · e</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Current (I)</strong>: Rate of flow of electric charge. SI unit: <strong>Ampere (A)</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>Charge (Q)</strong>: SI unit: <strong>Coulomb (C)</strong>. Electron charge e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.</li>
            <li>1 Ampere = 1 Coulomb / 1 Second.</li>
            <li>Measured by an <strong>Ammeter</strong> connected in <strong>series</strong> (very low resistance).</li>
            <li>Conventional current flows from <strong>positive to negative</strong> terminal (opposite to electron flow).</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🔋 Electric Potential &amp; Potential Difference</h3>
          <p class="formula">V = W / Q</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Potential Difference (V)</strong>: Work done (W) in moving a unit positive charge (Q) from one point to another.</li>
            <li>SI unit: <strong>Volt (V)</strong>. 1 V = 1 Joule / 1 Coulomb.</li>
            <li>Measured by a <strong>Voltmeter</strong> connected in <strong>parallel</strong> across the component (very high resistance).</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>📐 Ohm's Law &amp; Resistance</h3>
          <p class="formula">V = I · R &nbsp;⟹&nbsp; R = V / I</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Ohm's Law</strong>: Potential difference across a conductor is directly proportional to current flowing through it, provided temperature remains constant.</li>
            <li><strong>Resistance (R)</strong>: Property of a conductor to oppose flow of charge. SI unit: <strong>Ohm (Ω)</strong>.</li>
            <li>1 Ω = 1 Volt / 1 Ampere.</li>
            <li>V-I graph is a <strong>straight line passing through the origin</strong>. Its slope equals resistance (R).</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🧵 Factors Affecting Resistance &amp; Resistivity</h3>
          <p class="formula">R = ρ · (l / A)</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li>Directly proportional to length (R ∝ l).</li>
            <li>Inversely proportional to area of cross-section (R ∝ 1/A).</li>
            <li><strong>Resistivity (ρ)</strong>: Characteristic property of the material. SI unit: <strong>Ω·m</strong>.</li>
            <li>Alloys (e.g., Nichrome) have higher resistivity than pure metals and do not oxidize easily at high temperatures; used in heating elements.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🔀 Resistor Combinations</h3>
          <p class="formula">Series: R<sub>s</sub> = R₁ + R₂ + R₃<br/>Parallel: 1/R<sub>p</sub> = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + 1/R₃</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Series</strong>: Current ($I$) remains same; Voltage divides ($V = V_1 + V_2$). Equivalent resistance increases.</li>
            <li><strong>Parallel</strong>: Voltage ($V$) remains same; Current divides ($I = I_1 + I_2$). Equivalent resistance decreases.</li>
            <li>Domestic wiring is always in <strong>parallel</strong> so each appliance gets full 220V and independent switches.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🔥 Heating Effect &amp; Electric Power</h3>
          <p class="formula">H = I² · R · t &nbsp;|&nbsp; P = V · I = I² · R = V² / R</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Joule's Law of Heating</strong>: Heat produced H ∝ I², H ∝ R, H ∝ t.</li>
            <li>SI unit of Power: <strong>Watt (W)</strong> (1 W = 1 V × 1 A = 1 J/s).</li>
            <li><strong>Commercial unit</strong>: 1 kWh = 1 unit = 3.6 × 10⁶ Joules.</li>
            <li><strong>Electric Fuse</strong>: Safety device with low melting point connected in series with the live wire.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Visual Circuit Diagrams Section -->
    <section class="section" id="visual-circuits">
      <div class="rules-header">
        <span class="eyebrow">Interactive Diagrams</span>
        <h2>Visual Circuit Analysis</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          Explore key electrical circuits and graphs required for CBSE Class 10 board examinations:
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Tab Switcher -->
      <div class="rules-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Circuit categories">
        <button class="button rules-tab-btn active" id="tab-btn-series-parallel" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="panel-series-parallel">
          🔀 Series vs. Parallel Circuits
        </button>
        <button class="button secondary rules-tab-btn" id="tab-btn-ohms-law" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-ohms-law">
          📈 Ohm's Law Circuit &amp; V-I Graph
        </button>
      </div>

      <!-- Panel 1: Series vs Parallel -->
      <div class="rules-panel" id="panel-series-parallel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-btn-series-parallel">
        <div class="grid two rules-grid">
          <!-- Series Card -->
          <article class="card rule-card">
            <div class="rule-badge">Series Circuit · End-to-End Connection</div>
            <div class="rule-diagram">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 360 210" class="ray-svg" aria-label="Series Circuit Diagram">
                <rect width="360" height="210" rx="8" fill="#f8fafc" />
                <!-- Wires -->
                <path d="M 50 160 L 50 60 L 100 60" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none" />
                <!-- R1 -->
                <path d="M 100 60 l 6 -10 l 12 20 l 12 -20 l 12 20 l 12 -20 l 6 10" stroke="#b8561c" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none" />
                <text x="130" y="40" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#b8561c">R₁</text>
                <!-- Wire between R1 & R2 -->
                <line x1="160" y1="60" x2="200" y2="60" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5" />
                <!-- R2 -->
                <path d="M 200 60 l 6 -10 l 12 20 l 12 -20 l 12 20 l 12 -20 l 6 10" stroke="#b8561c" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none" />
                <text x="230" y="40" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#b8561c">R₂</text>
                <!-- Wires to battery -->
                <path d="M 260 60 L 310 60 L 310 160 L 220 160" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none" />
                <path d="M 50 160 L 140 160" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none" />
                <!-- Battery at bottom -->
                <line x1="140" y1="145" x2="140" y2="175" stroke="#0f4c5c" stroke-width="3.5" />
                <line x1="152" y1="152" x2="152" y2="168" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2" />
                <line x1="164" y1="145" x2="164" y2="175" stroke="#0f4c5c" stroke-width="3.5" />
                <line x1="176" y1="152" x2="176" y2="168" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2" />
                <line x1="176" y1="160" x2="220" y2="160" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5" />
                <text x="130" y="142" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#0f4c5c">+</text>
                <text x="182" y="142" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#334155">−</text>
                <text x="158" y="195" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#0f4c5c">Battery (V)</text>
                <!-- Current arrows -->
                <path d="M 50 100 l -4 8 l 8 0 z" fill="#b8561c" />
                <text x="35" y="105" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#b8561c">I</text>
                <path d="M 310 110 l -4 -8 l 8 0 z" fill="#b8561c" />
                <text x="325" y="110" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#b8561c">I</text>
              </svg>
            </div>
            <div class="rule-body">
              <h4>Same Current (I), Voltage Divides</h4>
              <p>In a series circuit, there is only one continuous path for charge. Current <strong>I</strong> remains identical through all resistors, while total voltage divides: <strong>V = V₁ + V₂</strong>. Equivalent resistance is <strong>R<sub>s</sub> = R₁ + R₂</strong>.</p>
            </div>
          </article>

          <!-- Parallel Card -->
          <article class="card rule-card">
            <div class="rule-badge">Parallel Circuit · Common Terminals</div>
            <div class="rule-diagram">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 360 210" class="ray-svg" aria-label="Parallel Circuit Diagram">
                <rect width="360" height="210" rx="8" fill="#f8fafc" />
                <!-- Main loop -->
                <path d="M 45 160 L 45 95 L 90 95" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none" />
                <path d="M 270 95 L 315 95 L 315 160 L 220 160" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none" />
                <path d="M 45 160 L 140 160" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none" />
                <!-- Junction nodes -->
                <circle cx="90" cy="95" r="4.5" fill="#0f4c5c" />
                <circle cx="270" cy="95" r="4.5" fill="#0f4c5c" />
                <!-- Branch 1 (Top R1) -->
                <path d="M 90 95 L 90 55 L 140 55" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2" fill="none" />
                <path d="M 140 55 l 6 -10 l 12 20 l 12 -20 l 12 20 l 12 -20 l 6 10" stroke="#b8561c" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none" />
                <path d="M 200 55 L 270 55 L 270 95" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2" fill="none" />
                <text x="170" y="38" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#b8561c">R₁ (I₁)</text>
                <!-- Branch 2 (Bottom R2) -->
                <path d="M 90 95 L 90 125 L 140 125" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2" fill="none" />
                <path d="M 140 125 l 6 -10 l 12 20 l 12 -20 l 12 20 l 12 -20 l 6 10" stroke="#0f4c5c" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none" />
                <path d="M 200 125 L 270 125 L 270 95" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2" fill="none" />
                <text x="170" y="148" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#0f4c5c">R₂ (I₂)</text>
                <!-- Battery at bottom -->
                <line x1="140" y1="145" x2="140" y2="175" stroke="#0f4c5c" stroke-width="3.5" />
                <line x1="152" y1="152" x2="152" y2="168" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2" />
                <line x1="164" y1="145" x2="164" y2="175" stroke="#0f4c5c" stroke-width="3.5" />
                <line x1="176" y1="152" x2="176" y2="168" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2" />
                <line x1="176" y1="160" x2="220" y2="160" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5" />
                <text x="130" y="142" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#0f4c5c">+</text>
                <text x="182" y="142" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#334155">−</text>
                <text x="158" y="195" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#0f4c5c">Battery (V)</text>
              </svg>
            </div>
            <div class="rule-body">
              <h4>Same Voltage (V), Current Divides</h4>
              <p>In parallel, components connect across common junction points. Potential difference <strong>V</strong> is identical across each branch, while current branches: <strong>I = I₁ + I₂</strong>. <strong>1/R<sub>p</sub> = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂</strong>.</p>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Panel 2: Ohm's Law & V-I Graph -->
      <div class="rules-panel" id="panel-ohms-law" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-btn-ohms-law" hidden>
        <div class="grid two rules-grid">
          <!-- Circuit Card -->
          <article class="card rule-card">
            <div class="rule-badge">Ohm's Law Verification Circuit</div>
            <div class="rule-diagram">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 360 210" class="ray-svg" aria-label="Ohm's Law Circuit Diagram">
                <rect width="360" height="210" rx="8" fill="#f8fafc" />
                <!-- Loop -->
                <path d="M 40 160 L 40 60 L 100 60" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none" />
                <!-- Resistor -->
                <path d="M 100 60 l 6 -10 l 12 20 l 12 -20 l 12 20 l 12 -20 l 6 10" stroke="#b8561c" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none" />
                <text x="130" y="42" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#b8561c">Resistor (R)</text>
                <!-- Voltmeter in parallel across R -->
                <path d="M 95 60 L 95 25 L 125 25" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-dasharray="3 3" fill="none" />
                <circle cx="140" cy="25" r="14" fill="#fff" stroke="#0f4c5c" stroke-width="2" />
                <text x="140" y="29" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#0f4c5c">V</text>
                <path d="M 154 25 L 185 25 L 185 60" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-dasharray="3 3" fill="none" />
                <!-- Ammeter in series -->
                <line x1="160" y1="60" x2="200" y2="60" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5" />
                <circle cx="220" cy="60" r="14" fill="#fff" stroke="#b8561c" stroke-width="2" />
                <text x="220" y="64" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#b8561c">A</text>
                <!-- Rheostat / battery / key -->
                <path d="M 234 60 L 310 60 L 310 160 L 240 160" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none" />
                <path d="M 40 160 L 120 160" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none" />
                <!-- Rheostat -->
                <path d="M 120 160 l 4 -6 l 8 12 l 8 -12 l 8 12 l 8 -12 l 4 6" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2" fill="none" />
                <path d="M 130 185 L 155 145" stroke="#0f4c5c" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#m1-arr-out)" />
                <text x="140" y="200" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" fill="#334155">Rheostat (Rh)</text>
                <!-- Battery -->
                <line x1="180" y1="148" x2="180" y2="172" stroke="#0f4c5c" stroke-width="3" />
                <line x1="190" y1="154" x2="190" y2="166" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2" />
                <line x1="190" y1="160" x2="240" y2="160" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2" />
                <text x="185" y="140" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" fill="#0f4c5c">Cell</text>
              </svg>
            </div>
            <div class="rule-body">
              <h4>Ammeter in Series, Voltmeter in Parallel</h4>
              <p>The ammeter measures current flowing through the circuit (placed in series). The voltmeter measures potential drop across resistor $R$ (placed in parallel).</p>
            </div>
          </article>

          <!-- Graph Card -->
          <article class="card rule-card">
            <div class="rule-badge">V-I Characteristic Graph</div>
            <div class="rule-diagram">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 360 210" class="ray-svg" aria-label="V-I Graph for Ohm's Law">
                <rect width="360" height="210" rx="8" fill="#f8fafc" />
                <!-- Grid lines -->
                <line x1="50" y1="130" x2="310" y2="130" stroke="#e2e8f0" stroke-width="1" />
                <line x1="50" y1="90" x2="310" y2="90" stroke="#e2e8f0" stroke-width="1" />
                <line x1="50" y1="50" x2="310" y2="50" stroke="#e2e8f0" stroke-width="1" />
                <line x1="130" y1="30" x2="130" y2="170" stroke="#e2e8f0" stroke-width="1" />
                <line x1="210" y1="30" x2="210" y2="170" stroke="#e2e8f0" stroke-width="1" />
                <line x1="290" y1="30" x2="290" y2="170" stroke="#e2e8f0" stroke-width="1" />
                <!-- Axes -->
                <line x1="50" y1="170" x2="320" y2="170" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#m1-arr-out)" />
                <text x="325" y="174" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#334155">I (A)</text>
                <line x1="50" y1="170" x2="50" y2="25" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#m1-arr-out)" />
                <text x="45" y="20" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#334155">V (V)</text>
                <!-- Origin -->
                <text x="40" y="184" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#64748b">O</text>
                <!-- Linear Graph -->
                <line x1="50" y1="170" x2="280" y2="45" stroke="#b8561c" stroke-width="3" />
                <!-- Slope indicator triangle -->
                <path d="M 130 127 L 210 127 L 210 83" stroke="#0f4c5c" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-dasharray="3 3" fill="none" />
                <text x="170" y="142" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" fill="#0f4c5c">ΔI</text>
                <text x="225" y="108" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" fill="#0f4c5c">ΔV</text>
                <!-- Equation label -->
                <rect x="180" y="45" width="135" height="34" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#b8561c" stroke-width="1.5" />
                <text x="247" y="66" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#b8561c">Slope = ΔV / ΔI = R</text>
              </svg>
            </div>
            <div class="rule-body">
              <h4>Straight Line Through Origin: V ∝ I</h4>
              <p>For an ohmic conductor at constant temperature, the V-I graph is a straight line through the origin. The <strong>slope of the line gives the resistance (R)</strong>.</p>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Formula Reference Table -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Formula &amp; SI Units Quick Reference</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap card">
        <table>
          <caption>Essential Electricity Formulas for Board Exams</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Symbol</th><th>Standard Formula</th><th>SI Unit</th><th>Key Note</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Electric Current</td><td>I</td><td>I = Q / t</td><td>Ampere (A)</td><td>Measured in series by Ammeter</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Potential Difference</td><td>V</td><td>V = W / Q</td><td>Volt (V)</td><td>Measured in parallel by Voltmeter</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Ohm's Law</td><td>R</td><td>R = V / I</td><td>Ohm (Ω)</td><td>Valid at constant temperature</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Resistance &amp; Resistivity</td><td>R, ρ</td><td>R = ρ · l / A</td><td>ρ in Ω·m</td><td>If wire is doubled on itself, A doubles &amp; l halves</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Series Combination</td><td>R<sub>s</sub></td><td>R<sub>s</sub> = R₁ + R₂ + …</td><td>Ohm (Ω)</td><td>Current is same; equivalent R is maximum</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Parallel Combination</td><td>R<sub>p</sub></td><td>1/R<sub>p</sub> = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂</td><td>Ohm (Ω)</td><td>Voltage is same; equivalent R is minimum</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Joule's Heating</td><td>H</td><td>H = I² · R · t</td><td>Joule (J)</td><td>Heat produced in conductor</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Electric Power</td><td>P</td><td>P = V · I = I²R = V²/R</td><td>Watt (W)</td><td>1 kW = 1000 W</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Commercial Energy</td><td>E</td><td>E = P · t</td><td>Kilowatt-hour (kWh)</td><td>1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ J (1 Unit)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Video Curriculum Alignment & All Discussed Questions Section -->
    <section class="section" id="video-masterclass">
      <div class="video-section-header">
        <span class="eyebrow">Video Curriculum Alignment</span>
        <h2>⚡ Complete Electricity One-Shot: Topics &amp; Questions Discussed</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          Comprehensive step-by-step solutions to every question and numerical problem discussed in the complete Class 10 Electricity One-Shot lecture (<a href="https://youtu.be/uhKPG_LErRc" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Manocha Academy One-Shot</a>), organized by chapter timestamps:
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="video-modules-grid">
        <!-- Module 1 -->
        <article class="card video-module-card">
          <div class="module-header">
            <span class="module-time">⏱ 00:00 – 18:11</span>
            <h3>1. Electric Circuit, Current, Charge &amp; Potential Difference</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="module-summary">
            <p><strong>Core Theory:</strong> Charge quantization Q = n · e (e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C). Electric current I = Q / t (Ampere). Conventional current flows positive-to-negative; electrons flow negative-to-positive. Ammeter (very low resistance) connects in <strong>series</strong>. Potential difference V = W / Q (Volt). Voltmeter (very high resistance) connects in <strong>parallel</strong>.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-list">
            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q1</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>Calculate the number of electrons constituting one Coulomb of electric charge.</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p>Given: Total charge Q = 1 C, charge on one electron e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.</p>
                  <p>Formula: Q = n · e ⟹ n = (Q / e) = [1 / (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹)] = (10¹⁹ / 1.6) = 6.25 × 10¹⁸ electrons.</p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> 6.25 × 10¹⁸ electrons make up 1 Coulomb of charge.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q2</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>A current of 0.5 A is drawn by a filament of an electric bulb for 10 minutes. Find the amount of electric charge that flows through the circuit.</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p>Given: Current I = 0.5 A, Time t = 10 min = 10 × 60 = 600 s.</p>
                  <p>Formula: Q = I × t = 0.5 A × 600 s = 300 C.</p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Electric charge Q = 300 Coulombs.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q3</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>How much work is done in moving a charge of 2 C across two points having a potential difference of 12 V?</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p>Given: Charge Q = 2 C, Potential difference V = 12 V.</p>
                  <p>Formula: V = (W / Q) ⟹ W = V × Q = 12 V × 2 C = 24 J.</p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Work done W = 24 Joules.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q4</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>Why is an ammeter always connected in series and a voltmeter in parallel in an electric circuit?</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p>• <strong>Ammeter:</strong> Has extremely low resistance. Connecting it in series ensures all circuit current flows through it without noticeably changing total circuit resistance or reducing current.</p>
                  <p>• <strong>Voltmeter:</strong> Has extremely high resistance. Connecting it in parallel across two points ensures it measures the voltage drop without diverting significant current away from the main branch.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <!-- Module 2 -->
        <article class="card video-module-card">
          <div class="module-header">
            <span class="module-time">⏱ 18:11 – 39:59</span>
            <h3>2. Ohm's Law, Resistance &amp; V-I Characteristic Graph</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="module-summary">
            <p><strong>Core Theory:</strong> Potential difference V ∝ I at constant temperature (V = IR). Resistance R = V/I (Ω). 1 Ω = 1 V / 1 A. In a V-I graph, a straight line through the origin proves Ohm's law; its slope (Δ V / Δ I) equals Resistance R. If plotted as an I-V graph, slope equals 1/R. A rheostat provides variable resistance.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-list">
            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q5</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>How much current will an electric bulb draw from a 220 V source if the resistance of the bulb filament is 1200 Ω?</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p>Given: V = 220 V, R = 1200 Ω.</p>
                  <p>Using Ohm's Law: I = (V / R) = (220 / 1200) = (11 / 60) ≈ 0.183 A.</p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Current drawn I ≈ 0.18 A.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q6</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>The potential difference between the terminals of an electric heater is 60 V when it draws a current of 4 A from the source. What current will the heater draw if the potential difference is increased to 120 V?</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find heater resistance: R = (V_1 / I_1) = (60 V / 4 A) = 15 Ω.</p>
                  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Calculate current at new voltage: I_2 = (V_2 / R) = (120 V / 15 Ω) = 8 A.</p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Current increases to 8 A (doubling voltage doubles current at constant resistance).</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q7</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>Two conductors A and B are tested and their V-I graphs are straight lines making angles of 30° and 60° with the current (I) axis. Which conductor has higher resistance?</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p>Slope of line on V-I graph (where V is vertical and I is horizontal) is tan θ = (Δ V / Δ I) = R.</p>
                  <p>For conductor A: R_A = tan 30° = (1 / √3) ≈ 0.577 Ω.</p>
                  <p>For conductor B: R_B = tan 60° = √3 ≈ 1.732 Ω.</p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Conductor B has greater resistance because the line with steeper slope towards the voltage axis represents higher resistance.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <!-- Module 3 -->
        <article class="card video-module-card">
          <div class="module-header">
            <span class="module-time">⏱ 39:59 – 01:01:13</span>
            <h3>3. Factors Affecting Resistance, Resistivity (ρ) &amp; Wire Stretching</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="module-summary">
            <p><strong>Core Theory:</strong> R = ρ (l / A) = ρ (l / π r²). Resistivity ρ is an intrinsic material property (unit: Ω·m). Alloys (Nichrome) have high resistivity and do not oxidize at high temperatures (used in heaters). Tungsten has a high melting point (3380°C, used in bulbs). Copper and aluminium have low resistivity (used in cables). <strong>Wire stretching rule:</strong> When a wire is stretched to n times its length, its volume remains constant (V = A · l), so area becomes A/n, making its new resistance R' = n^2 R, while resistivity ρ remains strictly constant.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-list">
            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q8</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>A wire of given material having length l and area of cross-section A has a resistance of 4 Ω. What would be the resistance of another wire of the same material having length l/2 and area of cross-section 2A?</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p>Initial wire: R_1 = ρ (l / A) = 4 Ω.</p>
                  <p>Second wire: R_2 = ρ (l/2 / 2A) = (1 / 4) (ρ (l / A)) = (1 / 4) × 4 Ω = 1 Ω.</p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> New resistance is 1 Ω.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q9</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>A cylindrical metallic wire of resistance R is stretched to double its original length. What will be its new resistance and new resistivity?</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p>• <strong>Resistivity:</strong> Unchanged! Resistivity depends only on material and temperature, not on length or thickness.</p>
                  <p>• <strong>Resistance:</strong> When stretched to double length (l' = 2l), its volume V = A × l is conserved. Therefore, cross-sectional area becomes A' = A / 2.</p>
                  <p>New resistance R' = ρ (l' / A') = ρ (2l / A/2) = 4 (ρ (l / A)) = 4R.</p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Resistance quadruples (4R); resistivity remains identical.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q10</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>A copper wire has diameter 0.5 mm and resistivity of 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ Ω·m. What will be the length of this wire to make its resistance 10 Ω? How much does the resistance change if the diameter is doubled?</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p>Radius r = (0.5 mm / 2) = 0.25 × 10⁻³ m = 2.5 × 10⁻⁴ m.</p>
                  <p>Area A = π r² = 3.14 × (2.5 × 10⁻⁴)^2 = 3.14 × 6.25 × 10⁻⁸ ≈ 1.963 × 10⁻⁷ m^2.</p>
                  <p>Length l = (R · A / ρ) = (10 × 1.963 × 10⁻⁷) / (1.6 × 10⁻⁸) ≈ 122.7 meters.</p>
                  <p>• If diameter is doubled, radius doubles and area quadruples (A' = 4A). Since R ∝ 1/A, the new resistance becomes (1 / 4)th = 2.5 Ω (decreases by 7.5 Ω).</p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Wire length = 122.7 m; resistance becomes 2.5 Ω.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <!-- Module 4 -->
        <article class="card video-module-card">
          <div class="module-header">
            <span class="module-time">⏱ 01:01:13 – 01:16:42</span>
            <h3>4. Resistors in Series &amp; Parallel Circuit Combinations</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="module-summary">
            <p><strong>Core Theory:</strong> <strong>Series:</strong> R_s = R_1 + R_2 + R_3. Current I is constant; voltage divides (V = V_1 + V_2 + V_3). Total resistance is greater than the largest individual resistance. <strong>Parallel:</strong> 1/R_p = 1/R_1 + 1/R_2 + 1/R_3. Voltage V is constant across branches; current divides (I = I_1 + I_2 + I_3). Equivalent resistance is smaller than the smallest individual resistance. Parallel is used in domestic circuits because appliances work independently at full 220 V.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-list">
            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q11</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>How can three resistors of resistances 2 Ω, 3 Ω, and 6 Ω be connected to give a total resistance of: (a) 4 Ω, (b) 1 Ω?</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p><strong>(a) To obtain 4 Ω:</strong> Connect 3 Ω and 6 Ω in parallel: (1 / R_p) = (1 / 3) + (1 / 6) = (3 / 6) = (1 / 2) ⟹ R_p = 2 Ω. Then connect R_p = 2 Ω in series with 2 Ω: R_total = 2 + 2 = 4 Ω.</p>
                  <p><strong>(b) To obtain 1 Ω:</strong> Connect all three in parallel: (1 / R_p) = (1 / 2) + (1 / 3) + (1 / 6) = (3 + 2 + 1 / 6) = (6 / 6) = 1 ⟹ R_p = 1 Ω.</p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) 3 Ω || 6 Ω in series with 2 Ω; (b) all three in parallel.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q12</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>A piece of wire of resistance R is cut into 5 equal parts. These parts are then connected in parallel. If the equivalent resistance of this combination is R', calculate the ratio R / R'.</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p>When cut into 5 equal parts, resistance of each piece is r = (R / 5).</p>
                  <p>In parallel: (1 / R') = (1 / r) + (1 / r) + (1 / r) + (1 / r) + (1 / r) = 5 × (1 / R/5) = (25 / R).</p>
                  <p>Inverting both sides: R' = (R / 25) ⟹ (R / R') = 25.</p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> (R / R') = 25.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q13</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>What is (a) the highest, and (b) the lowest total resistance that can be secured by combinations of four coils of resistance 4 Ω, 8 Ω, 12 Ω, and 24 Ω?</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p><strong>(a) Highest resistance (all in series):</strong> R_max = 4 + 8 + 12 + 24 = 48 Ω.</p>
                  <p><strong>(b) Lowest resistance (all in parallel):</strong> 1 / R_min = (1 / 4) + (1 / 8) + (1 / 12) + (1 / 24) = (6 + 3 + 2 + 1 / 24) = (12 / 24) = (1 / 2) ⟹ R_min = 2 Ω.</p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Highest = 48 Ω; Lowest = 2 Ω.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q14</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>An electric lamp of 100 Ω, a toaster of resistance 50 Ω, and a water filter of resistance 500 Ω are connected in parallel to a 220 V source. What is the resistance of an electric iron connected to the same source that takes as much current as all three appliances, and what is the current through it?</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p>Equivalent resistance: (1 / R_p) = (1 / 100) + (1 / 50) + (1 / 500) = (5 + 10 + 1 / 500) = (16 / 500) ⟹ R_p = (500 / 16) = 31.25 Ω.</p>
                  <p>Total current drawn from 220 V line: I = (V / R_p) = (220 / 31.25) = 7.04 A.</p>
                  <p>The electric iron replacing all three must have the same resistance 31.25 Ω and draws 7.04 A.</p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Resistance = 31.25 Ω; Current = 7.04 A.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <!-- Module 5 -->
        <article class="card video-module-card">
          <div class="module-header">
            <span class="module-time">⏱ 01:16:42 – 01:31:01</span>
            <h3>5. Heating Effect of Electric Current &amp; Joule's Law</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="module-summary">
            <p><strong>Core Theory:</strong> Joule's Law: H = I²Rt (Joules). Also expressed as H = VIt = (V² / R)t. Heat is directly proportional to square of current (I²), resistance (R), and time (t). Applications: (1) Heating elements use Nichrome wire with high resistivity and non-oxidizing properties. (2) Electric bulbs use Tungsten filament (3380°C melting point) filled with Argon/Nitrogen. (3) Electric fuse is a safety wire with a low melting point, connected in series with the live wire.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-list">
            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q15</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>Why does the cord of an electric heater not glow while the heating element does?</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p>• The connecting cord is made of thick copper, which has very low resistance. By Joule's Law (H = I²Rt), negligible heat is produced in the cord, so it remains cool.</p>
                  <p>• The heating element is made of Nichrome (an alloy), which has exceptionally high resistance. A large amount of heat (H = I²Rt) is generated in it, causing it to glow red-hot.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q16</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>100 J of heat is produced each second in a 4 Ω resistor. Find the potential difference across the resistor.</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p>Given: Rate of heat (H / t) = 100 J/s = 100 W, Resistance R = 4 Ω.</p>
                  <p>Formula: P = (H / t) = I² R ⟹ 100 = I² × 4 ⟹ I² = 25 ⟹ I = 5 A.</p>
                  <p>Potential difference V = I × R = 5 A × 4 Ω = 20 V.</p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Potential difference across resistor is 20 V.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q17</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>Compute the heat generated while transferring 96,000 C of charge in 1 hour through a potential difference of 50 V.</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p>Given: Charge Q = 96,000 C, Voltage V = 50 V, Time t = 1 h = 3600 s.</p>
                  <p>Work done / Heat generated: H = W = V × Q = 50 V × 96,000 C = 4,800,000 J = 4.8 × 10⁶ J.</p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Heat generated is 4.8 × 10⁶ Joules (or 4.8 MJ).</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q18</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>An electric iron of resistance 20 Ω takes a current of 5 A. Calculate the heat developed in 30 seconds.</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p>Given: R = 20 Ω, I = 5 A, t = 30 s.</p>
                  <p>Joule's Law: H = I² R t = (5)^2 × 20 × 30 = 25 × 20 × 30 = 15,000 J = 1.5 × 10⁴ J.</p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Heat developed = 1.5 × 10⁴ J (15 kJ).</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <!-- Module 6 -->
        <article class="card video-module-card">
          <div class="module-header">
            <span class="module-time">⏱ 01:31:01 – End</span>
            <h3>6. Electric Power, Bulb Ratings &amp; Commercial Energy Billing</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="module-summary">
            <p><strong>Core Theory:</strong> Power P = VI = I²R = (V² / R) (Watt). Commercial unit of electrical energy is 1 Kilowatt-hour (kWh), also called 1 "Unit": 1 kWh = 1000 W × 3600 s = 3.6 × 10⁶ Joules. For a rated appliance (V, P), its resistance is fixed: R = V² / P. When connected to a lower voltage V', power consumes P' = V'^2 / R.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-list">
            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q19</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>An electric bulb is rated 220 V and 100 W. When it is operated on 110 V, what will be the power consumed?</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p>Bulb filament resistance remains constant: R = (V² / P) = ((220)^2 / 100) = (48,400 / 100) = 484 Ω.</p>
                  <p>When operating at V' = 110 V: P' = (V'^2 / R) = ((110)^2 / 484) = (12,100 / 484) = 25 W.</p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Power consumed drops to 25 W (one-fourth of rated power).</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q20</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>An electric refrigerator rated 400 W operates 8 hours/day. What is the cost of the energy to operate it for 30 days at ₹3.00 per kWh?</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate total energy consumed in kWh:</p>
                  <p>E = P × t = 400 W × (8 h/day × 30 days) = 400 W × 240 h = 96,000 Watt-hours = 96 kWh.</p>
                  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Calculate bill cost:</p>
                  <p>Cost = 96 kWh × ₹3.00/kWh = ₹288.00.</p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Total electricity bill cost is ₹288.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q21</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>Which uses more electrical energy: a 250 W TV set in 1 hour, or a 1200 W toaster in 10 minutes?</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p>• <strong>TV set:</strong> E_1 = P_1 × t_1 = 250 W × 1 h = 250 Wh = 0.25 kWh = 250 × 3600 = 900,000 J.</p>
                  <p>• <strong>Toaster:</strong> E_2 = P_2 × t_2 = 1200 W × (10 / 60) h = 200 Wh = 0.20 kWh = 1200 × 600 = 720,000 J.</p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> The 250 W TV set consumes more energy (900 kJ vs 720 kJ) because it operates for a much longer duration.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q22</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>An electric heater of resistance 8 Ω draws 15 A from the service mains for 2 hours. Calculate the rate at which heat is developed in the heater.</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p>The <em>rate</em> at which heat is developed equals Electric Power: P = (H / t) = I² R.</p>
                  <p>P = (15 A)^2 × 8 Ω = 225 × 8 = 1800 J/s (or 1800 Watts).</p>
                  <p><em>(Note: Do not multiply by 2 hours because the question asks for rate of heat per second, not total heat).</em></p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Rate of heat development is 1800 J/s (1800 W).</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="qa-item">
              <span class="qa-q-num">Q23</span>
              <div class="qa-content">
                <p class="qa-q-text"><strong>Two electric bulbs rated 220 V, 100 W and 220 V, 60 W are connected in parallel to an electric mains supply. What current is drawn from the line if the supply voltage is 220 V?</strong></p>
                <div class="qa-solution">
                  <strong>Solution:</strong>
                  <p>Since connected in parallel across 220 V, both bulbs operate at their rated voltage:</p>
                  <p>Current in bulb 1: I_1 = (P_1 / V) = (100 W / 220 V) = (5 / 11) A ≈ 0.455 A.</p>
                  <p>Current in bulb 2: I_2 = (P_2 / V) = (60 W / 220 V) = (3 / 11) A ≈ 0.273 A.</p>
                  <p>Total line current I = I_1 + I_2 = (5 / 11) + (3 / 11) = (8 / 11) A ≈ 0.727 A.</p>
                  <p class="qa-ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Current drawn from the line is 0.727 A.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

