    opts: ["V ∝ 1/I", "V ∝ I", "V ∝ I²", "V is independent of I"],
    ans: 1,
    exp: "Ohm's Law states that V ∝ I at constant temperature, giving V = IR."
  },
  {
    q: "A cylindrical wire of length l and cross-sectional area A has resistance R. Another wire of same material, length 2l and area A/2 has resistance:",
    opts: ["R", "2R", "4R", "R/4"],
    ans: 2,
    exp: "R' = ρ(2l) / (A/2) = 4 · (ρl/A) = 4R."
  },
  {
    q: "The SI unit of electrical resistivity (ρ) is:",
    opts: ["Ohm (Ω)", "Ohm per metre (Ω/m)", "Ohm-metre (Ω·m)", "Volt per Ampere"],
    ans: 2,
    exp: "From R = ρl/A, ρ = R·A/l = (Ω · m²) / m = Ω·m."
  },
  {
    q: "Three resistors of 2 Ω, 3 Ω, and 6 Ω are connected in parallel. Their equivalent resistance is:",
    opts: ["11 Ω", "1 Ω", "0.5 Ω", "1.5 Ω"],
    ans: 1,
    exp: "1/Rp = 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/6 = (3 + 2 + 1) / 6 = 6/6 = 1. Therefore, Rp = 1 Ω."
  },
  {
    q: "Three resistors of 4 Ω, 6 Ω, and 10 Ω are connected in series with a 10 V battery. The current flowing through the 6 Ω resistor is:",
    opts: ["0.5 A", "1 A", "2 A", "0.25 A"],
    ans: 0,
    exp: "Rs = 4 + 6 + 10 = 20 Ω. Total current I = V / Rs = 10 V / 20 Ω = 0.5 A. In series, current is identical through every resistor."
  },
  {
    q: "Why are household electrical appliances connected in parallel rather than in series?",
    opts: [
      "Total resistance increases",
      "Each appliance operates at the same 220V potential and can be controlled independently",
      "Current through each appliance is identical",
